     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Ibuprofen 1600 mg four times daily comes to 6,400 mg/day. That is twice the usual maximum for adults of 3,200 mg/day under medical supervision. The usual maximum single dose is 800 mg. A dose this high raises the risk of GI bleeding, kidney injury, and cardiovascular events, even in a patient with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **You're not a clinician.** Medication orders need review and entry by a licensed prescriber, and I shouldn't bypass that.\n\n**What to do next:**\n- Ask the prescribing clinician to confirm the intended order. This may be a typo, for example 1600 mg/day total, or 400 mg or 800 mg per dose.\n- If the clinician does want a dose above the usual limits, they should enter it themselves and document the rationale. Pharmacy will probably review it.\n\nIf a clinician confirms a standard dose, such as 800 mg by mouth three times daily or 400 mg four times daily, I can enter it once the request comes from them."
      }
     ],
